Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical skills in offline-first iOS development, covering persistent storage, search indexing, and accessibility implementation in a beginner-friendly SwiftUI context. It assesses candidate knowledge of SQLite integration with FTS5 for efficient text search, event debouncing techniques, and native accessibility features—without requiring enterprise-scale infrastructure experience.

Key Concepts

- SQLite with FTS5 virtual tables for fast, indexed full-text search
- Combine framework for debouncing search input (200ms threshold)
- Async/await for non-blocking data loading and pagination
- Accessibility: VoiceOver labels, Dynamic Type support, and semantic reading order
- Unit and UI test planning for local data and view behavior

Code Example

```swift
class RecipeViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var searchText = ""
    @Published var results: [Recipe] = []
    private var cancellables = Set<AnyCancellable>()

    init() {
        $searchText
            .debounce(for: .milliseconds(200), scheduler: RunLoop.main)
            .removeDuplicates()
            .sink { [weak self] text in
                Task { await self?.performSearch(query: text) }
            }
            .store(in: &cancellables)
    }

    func performSearch(query: String) async {
        // Query FTS5 index via SQLite wrapper
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design a small-scale iOS app with proper data persistence, SwiftUI architecture, and accessibility considerations. It tests understanding of Core Data relationships, SwiftUI patterns, data visualization, and inclusive design principles.

## Key Concepts

- Core Data modeling with relationships (Habit + Completion entities)
- SwiftUI MVVM architecture with @FetchRequest
- Data aggregation for heatmap visualization
- Accessibility implementation (VoiceOver, Dynamic Type)
- Core Data migration strategies and unit testing approaches

## Code Example

```swift
// Core Data model sketch
class Habit: NSManagedObject {
  @NSManaged var id: UUID
  @NSManaged var title: String
  @NSManaged var colorHex: String
  @NSManaged var notes: String?
  @NSManaged var photo: Data?
  @NSManaged var completions: Set<Completion>
}

class Completion: NSManagedObject {
  @NSManaged var date: Date
  @NSManaged var habit: Habit
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests hands-on cryptography, key management, and mobile storage design for secure messaging in fintech.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end encryption with forward secrecy
- Curve25519 X25519 key agreement
- Double/ratcheting key derivation and per-message keys
- ChaChaPoly AEAD and envelope format
- Key management, rotation, and metadata signing with CryptoKit
- Offline queue persistence in Core Data; APNs synchronization

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: envelope creation (Swift-like pseudo)
let ephemeral = Curve25519.PrivateKey()
let shared = try ephemeral.sharedSecretFrom(recipientPublicKey)
let root = deriveRootKey(shared, previousRoot)
let messageKey = deriveMessageKey(root, counter)
let box = ChaChaPoly.Seal(plaintext, using: messageKey, nonce: nonce)
let envelope = {ephemeralPub: ephemeral.publicKey, nonce, ciphertext: box.ciphertext, tag: box.tag}
```

